### The 12 Audited & Retained Literature Sources

#### 1. Lau et al. (2024) — The Encyclopedia of Domains (TED)
- **Title:** Exploring structural diversity across the protein universe with The Encyclopedia of Domains
- **Authors/Year:** Andy M. Lau, Nicola Bordin, Shaun M. Kandathil, Ian Sillitoe, Vaishali P. Waman, Jude Wells, Christine A. Orengo, David T. Jones (2024)
- **DOI:** [10.1126/science.adq4946](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adq4946)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.adq4946`
- **Accessible Full Text / PDF:** [Lau_2024_TED_encyclopedia_domains_Science.pdf](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Lau_2024_TED_encyclopedia_domains_Science.pdf) (3.2 MB)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Deep-learning domain parsing (Chainsaw/Merizo) segmented 365M domains from 214M AFDB models; 77% matched existing CATH superfamilies. A strict attrition cascade (globularity, secondary structure count $\ge 6$, $\text{pLDDT}80 \ge 90$, Foldseek TM-align exclusion $>0.56$ at 60% coverage) collapsed 41.9M non-CATH clusters to just **7,427 putative novel folds** ($<0.02\%$).
- **Cited in P4:** Yes (`lau2024ted`).

#### 2. Yeo et al. (2025) — AFESM Structure Universe
- **Title:** Metagenomic-scale analysis of the predicted protein structure universe
- **Authors/Year:** Jingi Yeo, Yewon Han, Nicola Bordin, Andy M. Lau, Shaun M. Kandathil, Hyunbin Kim, Eli Levy Karin, Milot Mirdita, David T. Jones, Christine Orengo, Martin Steinegger (2025)
- **DOI:** [10.1101/2025.04.23.650224](https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.04.23.650224)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.04.23.650224`
- **Accessible Full Text / PDF:** [Yeo_2025_AFESM_structure_universe_bioRxiv.pdf](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Yeo_2025_AFESM_structure_universe_bioRxiv.pdf) (8.0 MB)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Merged AFDB and ESM Metagenomic Atlas into 821M structures (`AFESM`). Initial ESMFold models revealed zero novel domain folds; re-predicting 2.3M proteins with AlphaFold2 yielded only **one new fold**. However, it revealed **~12,000 novel multi-domain architectures**, demonstrating that metagenomic structural diversity is driven by modular domain recombination.
- **Cited in P4:** Yes (`yeo2025afesm`).

#### 3. Waman et al. (2025) — CATH v4.4 Expansion
- **Title:** CATH v4.4: major expansion of CATH by experimental and predicted structural data
- **Authors/Year:** Vaishali P. Waman, Nicola Bordin, Andy Lau, Shaun Kandathil, Jude Wells, David Miller, Sameer Velankar, David T. Jones, Ian Sillitoe, Christine Orengo (2025)
- **DOI:** [10.1093/nar/gkae1087](https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkae1087)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkae1087`
- **Accessible Full Text / PDF:** [Waman_2025_CATHv44_expansion_NAR.pdf](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Waman_2025_CATHv44_expansion_NAR.pdf) (8.4 MB)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** CATH expanded by absorbing ~90M predicted TED domains (+54% folds, +88% architectures). The authors explicitly caution: *"TED data comprises predicted structures, so these new folds and architectures remain hypothetical until experimentally confirmed."* Licenses P4's framing of candidates as "reference-relative candidates, not experimentally validated structures."
- **Cited in P4:** Yes (`waman2025cath`).

#### 4. Harteveld et al. (2024) — Generative Dark-Matter Folds
- **Title:** Exploring "dark-matter" protein folds using deep learning
- **Authors/Year:** Zander Harteveld, Alexandra Van Hall-Beauvais, Irina Morozova, Joshua Southern, Casper Goverde, Sandrine Georgeon, Stéphane Rosset, Michaël Defferrard, Andreas Loukas, Pierre Vandergheynst, Michael M. Bronstein, Bruno E. Correia (2024)
- **DOI:** [10.1016/j.cels.2024.09.006](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cels.2024.09.006)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cels.2024.09.006`
- **Accessible Full Text / PDF:** [Harteveld_2024_darkmatter_folds_deeplearning_CellSystems.pdf](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Harteveld_2024_darkmatter_folds_deeplearning_CellSystems.pdf) (8.4 MB)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Developed *Genesis* (SE(3) VAE + trRosetta) to generate de novo "dark-matter" folds unobserved in nature, validated by protease assays. Clarifies the crucial semantic boundary: designable fold space is physically possible, but natural microbial dark matter overwhelmingly reuses known fold topologies.
- **Cited in P4:** Yes (`harteveld2024darkfolds`).

#### 5. Durairaj et al. (2023) — New Families and Folds in AFDB
- **Title:** Uncovering new families and folds in the natural protein universe
- **Authors/Year:** Janani Durairaj, Andrew M. Waterhouse, Toomas Mets, Tetiana Brodiazhenko, Minhal Abdullah, Gabriel Studer, Gerardo Tauriello, Mehmet Akdel, Antonina Andreeva, Alex Bateman, Tanel Tenson, Vasili Hauryliuk, Torsten Schwede, Joana Pereira (2023)
- **DOI:** [10.1038/s41586-023-06622-3](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06622-3)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06622-3`
- **Accessible Full Text / XML:** [Durairaj_2023_new_families_folds_Nature.xml](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Durairaj_2023_new_families_folds_Nature.xml) (164 KB; Europe PMC PMC10584680)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Clustered AFDB into structural communities, identifying uncharacterized clusters and discovering genuine novel folds (such as the $\beta$-flower fold). Serves as the benchmark for large-scale structure clustering, showing that true fold discovery requires manual curation and high-confidence filtering.
- **Cited in P4:** Yes (`durairaj2023folds`).

#### 6. Xu & Zhang (2010) — TM-Score Normalisation & Significance
- **Title:** How significant is a protein structure similarity with TM-score = 0.5?
- **Authors/Year:** Jinrui Xu, Yang Zhang (2010)
- **DOI:** [10.1093/bioinformatics/btq066](https://doi.org/10.1093/bioinformatics/btq066)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://academic.oup.com/bioinformatics/article/26/7/889/212628`
- **Accessible Full Text / PDF:** [Xu_Zhang_2010_TMscore_05_significance_Bioinformatics.pdf](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Xu_Zhang_2010_TMscore_05_significance_Bioinformatics.pdf) (398 KB)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Derived that $\text{TM-score} \ge 0.5$ has a $P$-value $< 5.5 \times 10^{-7}$ for sharing the same global fold. Rigorously formulated the role of $L_{\text{norm}}$: normalizing by query length ($L_Q$) measures global covering alignment, whereas normalizing by aligned length ($L_{\text{common}}$) measures local domain similarity. Direct mathematical origin for P4's qTM vs alnTM distinction.
- **Cited in P4:** Yes (`xu2010tmscore`).

#### 7. Orr & Bateman (2024) — ESMFold & AlphaFold on Spurious Proteins
- **Title:** Folding the unfoldable 2: using AlphaFold and ESMFold to explore spurious proteins
- **Authors/Year:** Ailsa K. Orr, Alex Bateman (2024)
- **DOI:** [10.1093/bioadv/vbag160](https://doi.org/10.1093/bioadv/vbag160)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://academic.oup.com/bioinformaticsadvances/article/4/1/vbag160/7693522`
- **Accessible Full Text / XML:** [Orr_Bateman_2024_Folding_unfoldable_2_BioinformaticsAdvances.xml](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Orr_Bateman_2024_Folding_unfoldable_2_BioinformaticsAdvances.xml) (93 KB; Europe PMC PMC13294457)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** ESMFold, AF2, and AF3 predict short spurious sequences from AntiFam with deceptively high pLDDT scores ($>70$). Discrimination between spurious and real proteins improves sharply for sequences $\ge 100$ aa. Directly justifies P4's gating rule ($\ge 100$ aa) and proves that uncalibrated ESMFold predictions on short fragments produce artificial novel fold claims.
- **Cited in P4:** **No** (Recommended new citation).

#### 8. Wells et al. (2024) — Chainsaw Domain Parser
- **Title:** Chainsaw: protein domain segmentation with fully convolutional neural networks
- **Authors/Year:** Jude Wells, Alex Hawkins-Hooker, Nicola Bordin, Christine Orengo, David T. Jones (2024)
- **DOI:** [10.1093/bioinformatics/btae296](https://doi.org/10.1093/bioinformatics/btae296)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://academic.oup.com/bioinformatics/article/40/5/btae296/58958105`
- **Accessible Full Text / XML:** [Wells_2024_Chainsaw_domain_segmentation_Bioinformatics.xml](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Wells_2024_Chainsaw_domain_segmentation_Bioinformatics.xml) (123 KB; Europe PMC PMC11256964)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Deep residual convolutional network predicting pairwise residue domain co-membership matrices, outperforming previous unsupervised parsers. Directly powers TED. Serves as the primary tool to segment P4 candidates into autonomous domains and verify that their sub-regions match known CATH folds.
- **Cited in P4:** **No** (Recommended new citation for Methods M.6).

#### 9. Lau, Kandathil & Jones (2023) — Merizo Coordinate Domain Parser
- **Title:** Merizo: a rapid and accurate protein domain segmentation method using invariant point attention
- **Authors/Year:** Andy M. Lau, Shaun M. Kandathil, David T. Jones (2023)
- **DOI:** [10.1038/s41467-023-43934-4](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-43934-4)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-023-43934-4`
- **Accessible Full Text / XML:** [Lau_2023_Merizo_domain_segmentation_NatCommun.xml](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Lau_2023_Merizo_domain_segmentation_NatCommun.xml) (108 KB; Europe PMC PMC10730818)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Uses Invariant Point Attention (IPA) trained on CATH and self-distilled on predicted AlphaFold2 structures for end-to-end domain segmentation from coordinates. Specifically identified in P4 Dossier 03 as the unrun tool that can turn architectural novelty from an inference into a direct measurement.
- **Cited in P4:** **No** (Recommended new citation).

#### 10. Edgar (2024) — Reseek & Foldseek Benchmarking
- **Title:** Protein structure alignment by Reseek improves sensitivity to remote homologs
- **Authors/Year:** Robert C. Edgar (2024)
- **DOI:** [10.1093/bioinformatics/btae687](https://doi.org/10.1093/bioinformatics/btae687)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://academic.oup.com/bioinformatics/article/40/12/btae687/7841961`
- **Accessible Full Text / XML:** [Edgar_2024_Reseek_remote_homologs_Bioinformatics.xml](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Edgar_2024_Reseek_remote_homologs_Bioinformatics.xml) (62 KB; Europe PMC PMC11601161)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Benchmarked Foldseek 3Di against SCOP40. Proved that Foldseek 3Di misses remote structural homologs and underestimates false-positive E-values by orders of magnitude on large databases. Directly challenges claims of novel folds based solely on Foldseek 3Di triage, justifying P4's coordinate-level validation screen and blinded known-fold calibration floor.
- **Cited in P4:** **No** (Recommended new citation).

#### 11. Lee et al. (2025) — Foldseek-Multimer
- **Title:** Rapid and sensitive protein complex alignment with Foldseek-Multimer
- **Authors/Year:** Jeongjae Lee, Michel van Kempen, Stephanie S. Kim, Cameron L. M. Gilchrist, Martin Steinegger (2025)
- **DOI:** [10.1038/s41592-025-02593-7](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-025-02593-7)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://www.nature.com/articles/s41592-025-02593-7`
- **Accessible Full Text / XML:** [Lee_2025_Foldseek_Multimer_NatMethods.xml](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Lee_2025_Foldseek_Multimer_NatMethods.xml) (95 KB; Europe PMC PMC11903335)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Extends Foldseek to multi-chain assemblies and complexes, formalizing TM-score evaluation across multi-component structures. Directly contextualizes the failure of rigid-body alignment metrics on multi-domain architectures with flexible linkers.
- **Cited in P4:** **No** (Recommended new citation).

#### 12. Zhang et al. (2023) — DPAM Domain Parser
- **Title:** DPAM: A domain parser for AlphaFold models
- **Authors/Year:** Jing Zhang, Qian Cong, Lisa N. Kinch, Jimin Pei, R. Dustin Schaeffer, Nick V. Grishin (2023)
- **DOI:** [10.1002/pro.4548](https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.4548)
- **Publisher URL:** `https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/pro.4548`
- **Accessible Full Text / PDF:** [Zhang_2023_DPAM_domain_parser_ProteinScience.pdf](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/03_structure/Zhang_2023_DPAM_domain_parser_ProteinScience.pdf) (2.0 MB)
- **Precise Claim / Limitation:** Uses AlphaFold Predicted Aligned Error (PAE) matrices and Dali to parse globular domains and assign them to ECOD. Shows that multi-domain models systematically yield low whole-chain TM-scores to single-domain templates even when each constituent domain is an established fold.
- **Cited in P4:** **No** (Recommended new citation).

